<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · island</p><h1 class="enc-title">Caroline Islands</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Carolines</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Caroline Islands are a widely scattered archipelago in the western Pacific north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-guinea">New Guinea</a>, including Yap, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chuuk">Chuuk</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pohnpei">Pohnpei</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosrae">Kosrae</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palau">Palau</a>. Their peoples built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chiefdom">chiefdoms</a> with monumental centres such as Nan Madol and Leluh. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> claimed them, sold them to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1899, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> and then the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a> governed them before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federated-states-of-micronesia">Federated States of Micronesia</a> and Palau became independent.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/caroline-islands.jpg" alt="Caroline Islands" width="320" height="344" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Caroline_Islands-map.gif" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Pohnpei, a high volcanic island of about 334 square kilometres in the eastern <b>Caroline Islands</b>, was probably settled around the start of the Common Era by people of the Lapita cultural tradition from the south-east Solomon Islands or Vanuatu.</blockquote><a href="/empire/saudeleur-dynasty">Saudeleur dynasty · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>That year Germany bought the <b>Caroline</b>, Palau and Northern Mariana Islands from Spain, and the Marshall Islands Protectorate, including Nauru, was merged in 1906.</blockquote><a href="/state/german-new-guinea">German New Guinea</a></li><li><blockquote>Between about 1250 and 1400 the chiefdoms of Kosrae, the easternmost of the <b>Caroline Islands</b>, were probably unified under a single paramount ruler, the Tokosra.</blockquote><a href="/state/kosrae-paramount-chiefdom">Kosrae paramount chiefdom</a></li><li><blockquote>Early in the First World War the Japanese navy seized Germany's island colonies north of the equator: the Marianas except Guam, the <b>Carolines</b>, Palau and the Marshalls.</blockquote><a href="/state/south-seas-mandate">South Seas Mandate</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1947 the United Nations placed the Marshall, <b>Caroline</b> and Northern Mariana Islands under American administration as the only strategic trust territory, giving the United States the right to fortify them.</blockquote><a href="/state/trust-territory-of-the-pacific-islands">Trust Territory of the Pacific Islands</a></li><li><blockquote>Yap, a cluster of high islands in the western <b>Caroline Islands</b>, was settled over 2,000 years ago.</blockquote><a href="/state/yapese-chiefdoms">Yapese chiefdoms</a></li></ul></section>
